    50	#    - The new solver introduces extra assumptions: every meaningful blue component must be a
    51	#      3-cell L arrow, each such arrow must have exactly one adjacent marker, active marker colors
    52	#      must appear on exactly two arrows, and those two arrows must face each other along a shared
    53	#      diagonal. The generator explicitly constructs only inputs that satisfy these assumptions.
    54	#
    55	# d) Placement legality enforced by the generator
    56	#    - Arrow bodies, marker cells, trigger cells, and output-only fills/rays are all placed on
    57	#      distinct cells.
    58	#    - No active blue fill or magenta ray overwrites any input pixel. This includes markers and
    59	#      distractor arrows.
    60	#    - Marker cells are attached to the tip side of an arrow, stay off all active diagonals and
    61	#      future rays, and touch only their own arrow in 4-neighborhood.
    62	#    - Blue components are kept 4-disconnected from one another so the solver can recover each
    63	#      arrow unambiguously.
    64	#
    65	# e) Visual observability
    66	#    - Humans can directly see the L-arrow cue and infer that the diagonal leaves the tip through
    67	#      the missing quadrant.
    68	#    - Repeated marker colors across distant arrows make the pairing label visible in a few
    69	#      examples; singleton colors act as visibly unmatched distractors rather than hidden state.
    70	#    - Because every sample has multiple active pairs, the rule "pair by repeated marker color,
    71	#      then connect arrow tips" is more plausible than any accidental one-off geometry heuristic.
    72	#
    73	# f) Visual cleanliness
    74	#    - Active diagonals do not overlap each other, and rays do not pass through any pre-placed
    75	#      input object.
    76	#    - Markers sit beside arrow tips instead of on the diagonal itself, so arrows, labels, path
    77	#      fills, and magenta triggers remain easy to distinguish.
    78	#    - Distractor arrows, when present, are placed on diagonal extensions outside the active
    79	#      segment, so they create a clean ambiguity about pairing without cluttering the active path.
    80	#
    81	# g) Change inferability
    82	#    - Marker colors do not transform into output colors; they only label which arrows belong
    83	#      together. Blue still draws the main diagonal and magenta still draws the perpendicular
    84	#      rays. This keeps the added symbolic load small and structurally supported.
    85	
    86	
    87	_LINE_COLOR = 1
    88	_TRIGGER_COLOR = 6
    89	_MARKER_COLORS = [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9]
    90	
    91	# === CFG: canvas and structural bounds ===
    92	_H_RANGE = (8, 25)
